Threshold-selection logic

Karthikeya:
Now let me explain how to choose the right threshold.

The long-term goal is to have at least one useful insight for almost everyone in the 195K population.

There will be significant overlap between the signals. We cannot fully understand those overlaps without performing multivariate analysis.

For now, we are doing univariate analysis—one signal at a time.

Based on the results I’ve seen, I think an uplift of approximately 30% to 50% is a reasonable target.

When I say uplift, I mean uplift over the overall portfolio propensity—for example, comparing something like 0.65 against 0.57.

If you are currently getting only 20% uplift, try adjusting the threshold to increase it to approximately 30%.

However, volume also matters.

For example, if a signal applies to only 2,500 customers, that is approximately 1.25% of the population. That is already a very small group.

In that situation, a 20% uplift may still be acceptable.

But if the signal has a reasonable volume—say 5,000 or 10,000 customers—then we should aim for at least 30% uplift.

I also don’t want to push uplift significantly beyond 50%, because doing that may require an overly strict threshold and reduce the volume too much.

So, in general:

30%–50% uplift: Preferred range
Around 20% uplift: Acceptable when the eligible volume is already very small
Above 50% uplift: Usually unnecessary if it significantly reduces volume
